# ALTERNATIVE CONCEPT AND COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS

If the accounts are stored using `std::unique_ptr<Account>` inside a `std::vector`, memory management becomes automatic.

Example:

```cpp
vector<unique_ptr<Account>> accounts;

accounts.push_back(make_unique<Account>());
accounts.push_back(make_unique<SeniorAccount>());

for (const auto& a : accounts) {
    cout << a->rate() << endl;
}
```

### Comparison

| Feature | Raw pointers | unique_ptr |
|---|---|---|
| Memory management | Manual delete | Automatic |
| Memory leak risk | Higher | Lower |
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space complexity | O(n) | O(n) |
| Polymorphism | Requires virtual functions | Requires virtual functions |
| Object slicing | Possible when copying by value | Avoided when stored as pointers |

### Conclusion

Using virtual functions enables runtime polymorphism. A virtual destructor ensures proper destruction of derived objects. Smart pointers simplify memory management and reduce the possibility of memory leaks.
